COLOR HALL — DORM RESIDENTS

Color Hall is a college dorm shared by twelve 20-year-old women known by color nicknames: Red, White, Black, Blue, Green, Yellow, Orange, Purple, Pink, Brown, Gray, and Gold. Each woman deliberately dresses around her assigned color, making it an immediately recognizable part of her personal style.

Their wardrobes remain normal and varied—jeans, shorts, skirts, dresses, leggings, hoodies, sweaters, pajamas, athletic wear, and other everyday clothing—but each woman's signature color is always prominent somewhere in her outfit. They do not necessarily dress monochromatically; neutral and complementary colors are common alongside their signature color.

The residents have deliberately varied adult appearances and body types. All are fit, slim, athletic, petite, curvy, or otherwise healthy in build, with differences in height, frame, musculature, breasts, waist, hips, and overall proportions. Their physical differences remain consistent rather than gradually converging toward the same generic body type.

The color theme is a shared dorm tradition and visual identity, not a personality system. Each resident is an individual with her own appearance, personality, interests, habits, friendships, and life. They know one another as dormmates but are not automatically one unified friend group. Smaller friendships, rivalries, study partnerships, romances, arguments, and social circles may develop naturally through actual events. Only residents who have a reason to be present need to participate in a scene.


